---
// Page 09 — /holiday-home-management-dubai/ · DOOR · Template B
// Format per spec: fee comparison table + break-even calculator.
import PageShell from '../../layouts/PageShell.astro';
import DataTable from '../../components/DataTable.astro';
import FaqList from '../../components/FaqList.astro';
import ValuationCrossLink from '../../components/ValuationCrossLink.astro';
import NeedsSource from '../../components/NeedsSource.astro';
import { byPath } from '../../lib/pages.js';

const page = byPath('/holiday-home-management-dubai/');

const answer =
  'Holiday home operators in Dubai charge 15–25% of gross booking revenue. That fee covers ' +
  'listing, pricing, guest handling and cleaning coordination, but not the DET permit, the ' +
  'tourism dirham or the furnishing. Short-let grosses more than a long-term tenancy and nets ' +
  'less once those costs come out.';

const faqs = [
  {
    q: 'How much do holiday home operators charge in Dubai?',
    a:
      '15–25% of gross booking revenue. The spread reflects what is bundled: a 15% fee ' +
      'usually excludes linen, cleaning and guest supplies, while a 25% fee usually includes ' +
      'them. Compare on what you pay in total, not on the headline percentage.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What does the management fee not cover?',
    a:
      'Typically the DET holiday home permit, the tourism dirham charged per occupied night, ' +
      'utilities, internet, the initial furnishing and any maintenance. Those sit with the ' +
      'owner regardless of the fee model.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Do I need a permit to short-let my Dubai property?',
    a:
      'Yes. A holiday home permit issued by the Department of Economy and Tourism is required ' +
      'before a unit is let on a short-term basis. Either you hold it, or a licensed operator ' +
      'holds it on the unit’s behalf.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Is short-letting more profitable than a long-term tenant?',
    a:
      'It grosses more and nets less, and which one wins depends on the occupancy actually ' +
      'achieved rather than on the nightly rate advertised. A long-term Ejari tenancy has ' +
      'lower revenue and near-zero variable cost; short-let has both higher.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Who is responsible if a guest damages the property?',
    a:
      'That is a contract question and it varies materially between operators. Read what the ' +
      'agreement says about damage deposits, insurance and who pursues a claim before signing ' +
      '— it is the term that differs most between two otherwise similar offers.',
  },
];
---

<PageShell
  page={page}
  h1="Holiday home management in Dubai"
  answer={answer}
  faqs={faqs}
>
  <h2>What do operators charge, and for what?</h2>

  <DataTable
    caption="Holiday home management fee models in Dubai"
    columns={['Model', 'Typical fee', 'Usually included', 'Usually excluded']}
    rows={[
      [
        'Commission, lower band',
        '15% of gross',
        'Listing, pricing, guest communication, check-in',
        'Linen, cleaning, consumables, maintenance',
      ],
      [
        'Commission, upper band',
        '25% of gross',
        'The above plus cleaning coordination and consumables',
        'Permit, tourism dirham, utilities, furnishing',
      ],
      [
        'Fixed monthly payment',
        '<span class="pending">[NEEDS SOURCE: published fixed-monthly-payment terms]</span>',
        'A set monthly sum to the owner regardless of occupancy',
        'Upside when occupancy is strong',
      ],
      [
        'Fixed fee',
        '<span class="pending">[NEEDS SOURCE: published fixed-fee rates]</span>',
        'A flat monthly management charge',
        'Everything variable',
      ],
    ]}
    source="Published Dubai holiday home operator fee structures"
  />

  <p>
    Only the commission band is filled, because it is the figure the market publishes. Named
    operators’ own rate cards belong in the empty rows rather than a plausible number:{' '}
    <NeedsSource what="published fee structures from named Dubai holiday home operators, including fixed-payment and fixed-fee terms" />
  </p>

  <h2>How do you compare two operators honestly?</h2>

  <p>
    Not on the percentage. A 15% fee that excludes cleaning, linen and consumables can cost
    more per occupied night than a 25% fee that includes them, and the gap widens with turnover
    — a unit doing many short stays pays for cleaning many times.
  </p>

  <p>Ask each operator for the same four numbers and compare those instead:</p>

  <ol>
    <li>The percentage, and precisely what it is a percentage <em>of</em> — gross booking value, or net of platform fees</li>
    <li>The cleaning charge per turnover, and who pays it</li>
    <li>What the owner is billed for separately in a normal month</li>
    <li>The occupancy the operator is forecasting, and what that forecast is based on</li>
  </ol>

  <p>
    Treat the fourth answer carefully. An occupancy forecast is a projection, and a projection
    is not a fact about your unit. What can be checked is what comparable units in the same
    building actually achieved.
  </p>

  <h2>What does the owner still pay for?</h2>

  <ul class="two-col">
    <li>The DET holiday home permit, per unit</li>
    <li>Tourism dirham, charged per occupied night</li>
    <li>Utilities, which the owner carries rather than the guest</li>
    <li>Internet and any subscriptions</li>
    <li>Initial furnishing to the standard the platform requires</li>
    <li>Service charge, unchanged from any other ownership model</li>
    <li>Maintenance and replacement, which runs faster under short-let use</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    The permit itself is the item people discover late. What it requires, what it costs and how
    often it renews is on{' '}
    <a href="/holiday-home-management-dubai/permit/">the Dubai holiday home permit page</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Where is the break-even against a long-term tenant?</h2>

  <p>
    At the occupancy rate where short-let net equals long-let net. Above it, short-let wins;
    below it, the Ejari tenancy does, and it does so with far less work. The mechanism is
    straightforward — gross nightly revenue times occupancy, less the management fee, cleaning,
    tourism dirham, utilities and vacancy — but the inputs are specific to the building and the
    season.
  </p>

  <p>
    The model is worked through in full, side by side against a standard tenancy, on{' '}
    <a href="/holiday-home-management-dubai/short-let-vs-long-let/">
      short-let versus long-term rental in Dubai
    </a>.
  </p>

  <h2>What do people get wrong about short-letting?</h2>

  <p>
    They compare gross short-let revenue against net long-let revenue. It is not a comparison;
    it is two different measurements. A unit grossing well on nightly rates can net below what a
    tenant on an annual contract would have paid, once the fee, the cleaning, the permit, the
    utilities and the empty nights are subtracted.
  </p>

  <p>
    Note also what this page will not tell you: what yield to expect. H1 2026 residential
    transactions fell 13.6% year on year and Q2 2026 saw both prices and rents decline. Anyone
    projecting a return into that is guessing, and the projection is the part of the pitch that
    should make you most careful.
  </p>

  <ValuationCrossLink
    before="If the numbers push you toward selling rather than letting, "
    anchor="start with what the unit is actually worth today"
    after="."
  />

  <FaqList faqs={faqs} />
</PageShell>
